The smallest complete example of ownership and binding working together:

```swift
struct CounterView: View {
    @State private var count = 0

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Count: \(count)")
            StepperControl(count: $count)   // pass a binding
        }
    }
}

struct StepperControl: View {
    @Binding var count: Int   // reads/writes the parent's state

    var body: some View {
        HStack {
            Button("-") { count -= 1 }
            Button("+") { count += 1 }
        }
    }
}
```

`CounterView` owns `count` via `@State`. `StepperControl` never owns it — it only ever sees a `Binding<Int>`, so tapping its buttons mutates the parent's storage directly, and both views re-render in sync because there's exactly one source of truth.

`@State` and most SwiftUI property wrappers are meant to be touched on the **main actor** — UI state, by definition, drives the main thread's rendering. Mutating `@State` from a background task without hopping back to main either silently fails to update the UI, or in stricter Swift concurrency checking, is a compile-time or runtime violation.

```swift
func loadData() async {
    let result = await networkCall()
    // Wrong: if networkCall() isn't main-actor-isolated,
    // this mutation may not land on the view as expected.
    self.items = result
}
```

The fix in modern Swift is to mark the view or the specific property `@MainActor`, or explicitly hop with `await MainActor.run { self.items = result }`. This is the same underlying issue as GCD-era "forgot to dispatch back to main" bugs, just enforced more strictly by the compiler now.
